From b70b7dfad1ba75a9ea642505ab50db32c72906fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:10:12 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Xbox 360: shaders written for the console
 (FCF_PERF_TEST=lean)

The PC shaders work everything out per pixel with hash noise. Measured on
the console with the whole map drawn: 10 microcode slots a pixel = 30 fps,
468 slots = 8.5 fps, 1184 slots = 5 to 9 fps, and the 2990-slot water
hangs the GPU. So about 0.2 ms a slot, and room for 40 to 50 at 60 fps.

xenon/lean_shaders.py holds a second set of fragment shaders for the same
programs, used by transform.py when FCF_PERF_TEST=lean:

  slots     PC    noise   lean
  world    1184    468    106  (road + walls + the rest, picked by vMat)
  ground     -      -      38  (the terrain mesh, its own program)
  road       -      -      55  wall 40, misc 37: for per-material draws
  sky        ?     485     59
  water    2990   1096     92
  car        ?     680    108
  part       ?     180     40  mark 29, ring 43

How: noise is two lookups in a 256 x 256 texture (two tiling four-octave
value noises, white noise, one smooth octave) made in game.cpp; the world
is lit and fogged per vertex, so its pixel shader is only the surface
pattern; the tone curve is a cubic under a square root (within 0.03 of
the ACES fit and gamma above the deepest shadows); powers are repeated
squaring. No shadows, reflections on the road, headlight cone or stars
yet: those come back one at a time against the frame rate.

The compiler turns a test on vMat into "work out every material, then
pick", and its branches on a uniform come out unoptimised, so there is
one program per kind of surface. The terrain mesh is all material 0 and
uses "ground" now. "road", "wall" and "misc" are ready for when the
props mesh is drawn in per-material index ranges; until then "world"
draws it whole.

Checked: all 14 programs compile with xsc (xsdk 9e6de98); the lean world
vertex shader computes position with the same instructions as the depth
prepass's; the default, flat and noise shader sets still build and the
default gfx_shader_data.h is byte-identical; the desktop game renders
byte-identically in 17 scenes. The lean GLSL was also run on desktop
OpenGL (scratch build) and compared with the PC shaders side by side on
all four tracks, at night, in rain and beside water. Not run on Xenia or
the console.

Three titles: lean.xex (whole game), lean_sky_behind.xex (the sky drawn
after the world with the depth test, so covered pixels cost nothing),
lean_23.xex (mask 23, to compare with perf_flat and perf_noise).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013Q43n15Cor7dLxrScuVETZ
